item message:2 message final at=2464 text="Mara kept the light because the sea kept her father. Twenty years ago he had rowed out after a flare that never came back, and the tower on Blackbar Point had needed someone who would not leave the stairs. She learned the lamp the way other people learn a kitchen: by touch, in the dark, with the wind arguing through the glass.\n\nEach evening she climbed the spiral with a cloth over her shoulder and a tin of oil in her hand. The steps were worn in the middle, polished by boots that had belonged to keepers before her. At the top the lantern room smelled of hot metal and salt. She trimmed the wick, wiped the lens until her own face vanished in the curve of it, and set the mechanism turning. The beam walked the water in a slow white circle. Ships answered with small lights of their own, polite and distant, and then they were gone.\n\nOn the third night of the November gale the fog came in so thick she could not see the railing. The horn took up its work, a low note that shook the floor under her boots. She stayed in the lantern room with a blanket and the logbook, writing the hour and the state of the sea the way the manual required. Somewhere past midnight the horn stuttered and died. The silence afterward was worse than the wind.\n\nMara went down to the engine room with a lantern and found the belt thrown and the motor hot enough to sting her palm through the glove. She had fixed it twice before. This time the spare belt was cracked along its length, the rubber gone white at the fold. She stood with it in both hands and listened to the tower creak. No ship would hear a light in that fog. They needed the sound.\n\nIn the store cupboard she found an old leather strap her father had used to carry coal, stiff and salted and still whole. She cut it, punched holes with a nail, and laced it through the pulleys with wire from the fence coil. Her fingers slipped. She swore at the machine the way she had heard him swear, without heat, as if the words were tools. When she threw the switch the motor caught, coughed, and settled into its old knock. The horn came back, late and a little flat, and filled the fog again.\n\nShe climbed to the light and stayed until the gray came up. A freighter passed close enough that she saw the rust on its bow and a man in a yellow coat lifting one hand. She lifted hers back, though he could not have seen her face. When the fog thinned she wrote the night in the log in her square hand: belt failed; repaired with leather strap; horn restored at 1:40. She did not write that she had been afraid. The book was for the next keeper, and fear did not help them.\n\nAt noon she slept in the chair by the kitchen stove. When she woke, the strap was still holding and the sea had gone the color of pewter. She made tea, carried it up, and stood with the cup warming her hands while the beam turned over a quieter water. The tower would need a proper belt. She would send for one. Until it came, the leather would do, and so would she, which was the whole of the work: to be the person who stayed when the dark came in and the machines forgot their duty."
